  "query_preview": "..The Excessive Rainfall Outlook through Thursday \ndepicts portions of southeast Arizona in a slight risk (level 2 of \n4) equating to a 25 percent chance of excessive rainfall leading to \nflash flooding within 15 miles of a point. Over the past 72 hours, \nwe've seen flowing washes and rivers after 1 to 2 inches of rain has \nfallen in some locales providing saturated soils and flashy \nantecedent conditions for any additional rainfall.\n\nFor today, the slight risk for excessive rainfall is centered on \nareas from the Tucson Metro eastward. As of this writing, showers \nand storms are already forming in moisture rich airmass with robust \ninstability and will likely become more widespread over the next few \nhours as a mesoscale convective system drifts north over Sonora \nMexico providing additional forcing. In this juicy environment, \nstorms this afternoon and evening will be capable of producing \nrainfall rates of 1-2 inches per hour with a slight chance for \nlocally higher rates under any of the stronger cells that cluster. \nFlash Flood Watch continues through 11 PM MST this evening. \nPotential impacts/hazards include urban and street flooding, water \nrunning through normally dry washes, and low water crossings \nbecoming inundated and impassable. Overnight some of the CAMs are \nshowing nocturnal activity continuing thus, chances for showers and \nstorms (20-50%) continue overnight. Something to keep an eye on.\n\nWhile tomorrow does still appear to be a relative down day, storms \ntoday and tonight will likely play a role in the coverage and/or \ntiming of storms tomorrow. That being said, any storms that form \nwill again have the potential for locally heavy rainfall rates \nupwards of 1 to 2 inches per hour. Slight risk for excessive \nrainfall resulting in flash flooding continues centered from the \nTucson Metro east.\n\nWednesday is looking to be another more active day with scattered to \nnumerous thunderstorms as an upper level disturbance moves north out \nof Sonora Mexico and across southeast Arizona. The disturbance will \nadd additional upper level support ultimately helping air rise in an \nenvironment with precipitable water content around 150-250% of \nnormal for this time of year. Storms will be capable of producing \nheavy rainfall rates, although the specific timing/positioning of \nthe disturbance relative to the best instability could drive where \nstorms specifically form initially and grow upscale.\n\nThursday: Similar to Tuesday in that it is still looking not quite \nas active as Wednesday as forcing from the disturbance that comes \nthrough on Wednesday will move out of the area. But with plenty of \nmoisture around, any storms that do form will again be capable of \nheavy rainfall. Slight risk for excessive rainfall resulting in \nflash flooding continues from Tucson eastward.\n\nFriday into this weekend: Drier air finally pushes into southeast \nArizona from the west as our flow aloft turns westerly. Chances for \nprecipitation (10-40%) continue, but with drier air aloft, we will \nsee less heavy rainfall. Looking like the best chances will remain \neast and south of Tucson.\n\n8 to 14 day outlooks (Mon Sep 21 to Sun Sep 27) \nTemperature: Leaning above normal, 44-45% chance \nPrecipitation: Likely above, 49-52% chance",
